# Web and Next.js Rules

## Stack
- Preserve the installed Node.js, Next.js, React, TypeScript, and package-manager versions.
- Use pnpm when `pnpm-lock.yaml` exists.
- Do not upgrade framework versions without explicit approval.
- Use the repository's established styling, data-fetching, API, and state-management patterns.

## Next.js
- When the repository uses the App Router, follow App Router conventions.
- Default to Server Components.
- Add `"use client"` only when browser APIs, event handlers, client state, or a client-only library require it.
- Keep secrets, privileged logic, database access, and server-only code out of Client Components.
- Use the existing route-handler, server-action, tRPC, REST, or GraphQL convention.
- Include loading, empty, error, and unauthorized states when applicable.
- Preserve semantic HTML, labels, keyboard navigation, focus handling, and visible focus states.

## Structure
- `src/app/` contains routes, layouts, route-level UI, and route handlers.
- `src/components/` contains reusable UI components.
- `src/lib/` contains shared utilities, validation, types, and safe business logic.
- `src/server/` contains server-only services, database logic, authentication helpers, and integrations.
- `prisma/` contains Prisma schema, migrations, and seed logic.
- `tests/` contains E2E tests, integration tests, fixtures, factories, and test setup.
Use the existing repository structure if it differs. Do not reorganize folders only to match this rule.

## Imports and performance
- Use configured path aliases such as `@/components`, `@/lib`, and `@/server`.
- Do not use relative imports that traverse more than one parent directory.
- Avoid circular dependencies.
- Avoid unnecessary client-side JavaScript, client state, and rendering work.
- Prevent layout shift by reserving space for media and asynchronous content.
- Do not introduce caching, memoization, virtualization, or optimization complexity without a concrete reason.